            {
              "id": "glog-2047d377-b6f0-4bf4-a2bb-389fa12063f8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detected Issue with C Function (memcpy)\" vulnerability in C++ arises when the `memcpy` function is used improperly, potentially leading to buffer overflows, data corruption, or undefined behavior. This vulnerability typically occurs when the destination buffer is not large enough to hold the data being copied, or when the source and destination overlap, which is not allowed by `memcpy`.\n\nIn the specific example `memcpy(p, &v, sizeof v)`, the vulnerability may occur if the buffer `p` is not large enough to accommodate the size of `v`. This can lead to overwriting adjacent memory, causing unpredictable behavior or security vulnerabilities.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Ensure Buffer Size**: Always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `memmove` if there is a possibility of overlapping memory regions, or `std::copy` in C++ for type-safe copying.\n3. **Boundary Checks**: Implement boundary checks to prevent buffer overflows.\n4. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect potential buffer overflows and other memory issues.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nTo fix the specific vulnerability sink, ensure that the destination buffer `p` is properly sized and consider using safer alternatives:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // Required for memcpy\n#include <algorithm> // Required for std::copy\n\n// Example fix using std::copy\nvoid safeCopy(void* p, const void* v, size_t size) {\n    // Ensure p is large enough to hold the data\n    if (p == nullptr || v == nullptr) return; // Check for null pointers\n\n    // Use std::copy for type-safe copying\n    std::copy(static_cast<const char*>(v), static_cast<const char*>(v) + size, static_cast<char*>(p));\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\n- `<cstring>`: Required for `memcpy`.\n- `<algorithm>`: Required for `std::copy`.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Buffer_Overflow)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible for anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS121708"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-9fb656b8-d4a4-48ee-9798-7a326c77d806",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-29541** affects the `tensorflow` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, available on PyPI. This vulnerability arises from a flaw in the implementation of the `tf.raw_ops.SparseCountSparseOutput` operation. The issue is due to a lack of validation on the input arguments, which can lead to a heap buffer overflow. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service (DoS) by crashing the application.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. The TensorFlow team has addressed this issue in later releases, so upgrading to the latest stable version is advised.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement additional input validation checks in your application to ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds and types.\n\n3. **Security Patches**: Regularly apply security patches and updates to your dependencies to protect against known vulnerabilities.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe TensorFlow team has fixed this issue in later versions by adding proper validation checks for the input arguments of the `tf.raw_ops.SparseCountSparseOutput` operation. If you are unable to upgrade, consider backporting the fix from the TensorFlow repository to your version. The fix involves adding checks to ensure that the input arguments are valid and within expected ranges before processing.\n\n### Relevant Links\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-787](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/787.html)\n\nThese resources provide additional information on secure coding practices and common software weaknesses that can help prevent similar vulnerabilities in the future."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "CVE-2021-29541/pkg:pypi/tensorflow@2.1.0"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
