              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2022-36027** affects the `tensorflow` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, available on PyPI. This vulnerability arises from improper input validation in certain TensorFlow operations, which can lead to undefined behavior, including potential crashes or execution of arbitrary code. The issue is particularly concerning in environments where untrusted data is processed, as it could be exploited by attackers to compromise the system.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: Update to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow release notes or security advisories for the specific version that addresses this issue.\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation and sanitization to ensure that only well-formed data is processed by TensorFlow operations.\n3. **Environment Isolation**: Run TensorFlow operations in a sandboxed or isolated environment to limit the impact of potential exploitation.\n4. **Regular Security Audits**: Conduct regular security audits and code reviews to identify and address potential vulnerabilities in your codebase.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability is not provided, general recommendations include:\n\n- Reviewing the TensorFlow GitHub repository for any patches or pull requests related to this CVE.\n- Applying any available patches or updates from the TensorFlow maintainers.\n- Contributing to the TensorFlow project by reporting any additional vulnerabilities or issues discovered during your review.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the top ten security risks for web applications, which can provide guidance on securing applications against common vulnerabilities.\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/): A quick reference guide for secure coding practices to help developers avoid common security pitfalls.\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\nThis vulnerability is related to **CWE-20: Improper Input Validation**. More information can be found on the [CWE website](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/20.html)."
