                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe vulnerability identified as **CVE-2021-37639** affects the `tensorflow-gpu` package, specifically version `2.1.0`, which is a part of the TensorFlow library. TensorFlow is an open-source platform for machine learning. This vulnerability arises from an issue in the way TensorFlow handles certain operations, potentially leading to a denial of service (DoS) or other unintended behaviors. The root cause is typically improper validation of input data, which can be exploited by an attacker to crash the application or execute arbitrary code.\n\n### Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Upgrade TensorFlow**: The most effective mitigation is to upgrade to a version of TensorFlow where this vulnerability is patched. Check the TensorFlow GitHub repository or PyPI for the latest stable release and update your dependencies accordingly.\n\n2. **Input Validation**: Implement strict input validation to ensure that all inputs to TensorFlow operations are within expected bounds and types. This can prevent malformed data from triggering the vulnerability.\n\n3. **Isolation**: Run TensorFlow processes in isolated environments (e.g., containers) to limit the impact of a potential exploit.\n\n4. **Monitoring and Logging**: Implement comprehensive monitoring and logging to detect unusual activities that may indicate exploitation attempts.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWhile the specific source code fix for this vulnerability would be implemented by the TensorFlow maintainers, a general approach to addressing such issues involves:\n\n- Adding checks to validate input data before processing.\n- Ensuring that all operations handle edge cases gracefully.\n- Using safe programming practices to avoid buffer overflows and other common vulnerabilities.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/): A list of the most critical web application security risks.\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.